.TH ENVS 1 2026-11-07 "environment-store 2.0" "User Commands"
.\" Hand-written reference page (D49). Keep it in step with the CLI:
.\" tests/docs/test_reference_pages.py checks commands, options and exit codes.
.SH NAME
envs, environment-store \- keep environment variables in named stores
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B envs
.RB [ \-c
.IR URL ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-project ]
.RB [ \-\-noinput ]
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ arguments ]
.br
.B envs
.RB [ \-h | \-\-help | \-\-version ]
.br
.B environment-store
.RI ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B envs
keeps environment variables in named
.IR stores .
A store maps variable names to string values and is either plaintext or
encrypted as a whole with AES\-256\-GCM, using a random key file or a
passphrase (Argon2id).
Stores live in a backend chosen by a connection string: a directory of JSON
or TOML files, an SQLite database or a PostgreSQL database.
.PP
Reading commands load one or more stores in a given order, merge them and
list, print, export or run a command with the result.
Writing commands change exactly one store, named with
.BR \-s .
.PP
.B environment-store
is the same program under its long name.
The CLI needs the
.B cli
extra, the PostgreSQL backend the
.B postgres
extra and the TUI the
.B tui
extra, for example
.B uv tool install \(dqenvironment-store[cli]\(dq
or
.BR "uvx \-\-from \(dqenvironment-store[cli]\(dq envs" .
Version 2.0 is not on PyPI yet: there these commands install the old 0.1 line,
which has no
.B envs
command.
Until 2.0 is released, add the repository URL, as in
.BR "uv tool install \(dqenvironment\-store[cli] @ git+https://github.com/mkaufhol/environment\-store@feature/v2\(dq" .
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
Global options come before the command name.
.TP
.BI \-c " URL" "\fR, \fP\-\-connection" " URL"
Connection string of the backend; overrides
.B ENV_STORE_CONNECTION
and
.B connection
in the project file.
There is no default: without a connection, every command that opens a store
fails.
Forms:
.BI json:// directory\fR,
.BI toml:// directory\fR,
.BI sqlite:/// relative.db\fR,
.BI sqlite://// absolute/path.db
and
.BR postgresql://user@host:5432/db [ ?schema=\fIname\fP ].
File and SQLite paths expand a leading
.BR ~ .
.TP
.B \-\-no\-project
Ignore the project file
.BR .envstore.toml .
.TP
.B \-\-noinput
Never prompt, even on a terminal.
A missing key or passphrase fails with exit code 4, a missing confirmation
(no
.BR \-y )
with exit code 1, and
.B set
reads a value only from stdin.
Meant for CI.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show the help of
.B envs
or, after a command name, of that command, and exit.
.PP
.BR \-h ,
.BR \-\-help ,
.BR \-\-version ,
.B keygen
and
.B completion
read neither the connection nor the project file.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS migrate
.B envs migrate
.PP
Create or upgrade the SQL schema of an SQLite or PostgreSQL backend.
SQLite schemas are created and upgraded automatically; on PostgreSQL an
older schema is only upgraded by this command, which an administrator can
also use for the first setup.
.SS ls-store
.B envs ls\-store
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.PP
List all stores and whether they are encrypted; needs no key.
With no stores, stdout stays empty and stderr names the resolved location.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print one JSON document.
.SS create-store
.B envs create\-store
.RB [ \-e
.RB ( \-k
.IR PATH | \-
.RB | " \-\-generate\-key"
.I PATH
.RB | " \-\-passphrase" )]
.I NAME
.PP
Create a new, empty store.
Store names match
.B ^[a\-z][a\-z0\-9_\-]{0,62}$
and must be unique in the backend.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-encrypt
Create an encrypted store; needs exactly one of
.BR \-k ,
.BR \-\-generate\-key ,
.BR \-\-passphrase .
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-key " \fIPATH\fP|\fB\-\fP"
Encrypt with this existing key file
.RB ( \-
reads it from stdin).
.TP
.BI \-\-generate\-key " PATH"
Encrypt with a new key file written to
.I PATH
(mode 0600, never overwritten).
.TP
.B \-\-passphrase
Encrypt with a passphrase: read from stdin when piped, else asked twice.
.SS rm-store
.B envs rm\-store
.RB [ \-y ]
.I NAME
.PP
Delete a store.
Needs no key, also when the store is encrypted.
Asks for confirmation on a terminal.
.TP
.BR \-y ", " \-\-yes
Do not ask for confirmation; required when stdin is not a terminal.
.SS cp-store
.B envs cp\-store
.RB [ \-\-to
.IR URL ]
.RB [ \-k
.IR PATH | \- ]
.RB [ \-\-plain " | " \-\-new\-key
.IR PATH | \-
.RB | " \-\-generate\-key"
.I PATH
.RB | " \-\-passphrase" ]
.I SRC DST
.PP
Copy a store under a new name, optionally into another backend.
The copy is encrypted like the source, with the same secret, unless one of
the mutually exclusive options
.BR \-\-plain ,
.BR \-\-new\-key ,
.BR \-\-generate\-key ,
.B \-\-passphrase
is given.
Fails if
.I DST
exists.
.TP
.BI \-\-to " URL"
Connection string of another backend; there
.I DST
may equal
.IR SRC .
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-key " \fIPATH\fP|\fB\-\fP"
Key or passphrase file of the source store,
.B \-
for stdin.
Default:
.BI ENV_STORE_KEY_ STORE\fR,
then
.B [keys]
in the project file, else a prompt on a terminal.
.TP
.B \-\-plain
Make an unencrypted copy; this is the way to decrypt a store.
.TP
.BR \-\-new\-key " \fIPATH\fP|\fB\-\fP"
Encrypt the copy with this existing key file.
.TP
.BI \-\-generate\-key " PATH"
Encrypt the copy with a new key file written to
.I PATH
(mode 0600, never overwritten).
.TP
.B \-\-passphrase
Encrypt the copy with a passphrase: read from stdin when piped, else asked
twice.
.SS mv-store
.B envs mv\-store
.RB [ \-\-to
.IR URL ]
.RB [ \-k
.IR PATH | \- ]
.RB [ \-\-plain " | " \-\-new\-key
.IR PATH | \-
.RB | " \-\-generate\-key"
.I PATH
.RB | " \-\-passphrase" ]
.I SRC DST
.PP
Rename a store, or move it into another backend.
Takes the options of
.BR cp\-store :
.BR \-\-to ,
.BR \-k ", " \-\-key ,
.BR \-\-plain ,
.BR \-\-new\-key ,
.BR \-\-generate\-key ,
.BR \-\-passphrase .
The source is deleted only after the copy is written, so a failure never
leaves zero copies.
Renaming an encrypted store re\-encrypts it, because the ciphertext is bound
to the store name, and therefore needs its key.
.SS ls
.B envs ls
.RB [ \-v ]
.RB [ \-\-source ]
.RB [ \-\-first\-wins ]
.RB [ \-k
.IR STORE = PATH ]...
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.RI [ STORE ...]
.PP
List the variable names of one or more stores, merged in load order (see
.BR "MERGE ORDER" ).
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-values
Also show the values.
.TP
.B \-\-source
Show the store each winning value comes from; also works without
.BR \-v .
.TP
.B \-\-first\-wins
The first store wins instead of the last (overrides
.BR ENV_STORE_ORDERED_OVERWRITE ).
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-key " \fISTORE\fP=\fIPATH\fP"
Key or passphrase file of a store,
.B \-
for stdin; repeatable.
With one store,
.BI \-k " PATH"
is enough.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Print one JSON document.
.SS get
.B envs get
.RB [ \-\-first\-wins ]
.RB [ \-k
.IR STORE = PATH ]...
.RB [ \-\-json ]
.I KEY
.RI [ STORE ...]
.PP
Print the value of variable
.IR KEY ;
with several stores, the value that wins.
The raw value is printed without a trailing newline unless stdout is a
terminal, so
.B $(envs get NAME store)
works for multi\-line values too.
Exit code 2 if no store has the variable.
Options as for
.BR ls :
.BR \-\-first\-wins ,
.BR \-k ", " \-\-key ,
.BR \-\-json .
.SS set
.B envs set
.B \-s
.I STORE
.RB [ \-o ]
.RB [ \-k
.IR PATH | \- ]
.IR KEY = VALUE ...
.br
.B envs set
.B \-s
.I STORE
.RB [ \-o ]
.RB [ \-k
.IR PATH | \- ]
.I KEY
.PP
Set one or more variables in one store, in a single write.
.IR KEY = VALUE
pairs are refused for encrypted stores, because command lines end up in the
shell history: give one
.I KEY
and pipe its value (one trailing newline is stripped) or type it at the
hidden prompt, which asks twice.
Empty stdin is an error, so a variable is never blanked by accident.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-store " \fISTORE\fP"
The store to change (required).
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-overwrite
Overwrite existing variables; otherwise an existing variable is an error and
nothing is written.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-key " \fIPATH\fP|\fB\-\fP"
Key or passphrase file of the store,
.B \-
for stdin.
Default:
.BI ENV_STORE_KEY_ STORE\fR,
then
.B [keys]
in the project file, else a prompt on a terminal.
.SS rm
.B envs rm
.B \-s
.I STORE
.RB [ \-k
.IR PATH | \- ]
.IR KEY ...
.PP
Delete one or more variables from one store, in a single write.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-store " \fISTORE\fP"
The store to change (required).
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-key " \fIPATH\fP|\fB\-\fP"
Key or passphrase file of the store,
.B \-
for stdin.
.SS import
.B envs import
.B \-s
.I STORE
.RB [ \-f
.IR PATH | \- ]
.RB [ \-t
.BR dotenv | json | toml ]
.RB [ \-o ]
.RB [ \-k
.IR PATH | \- ]
.PP
Fill an existing store from a dotenv, JSON or TOML file, or from stdin.
JSON and TOML input must be a flat object of string values.
Dotenv input is imported literally;
.B ${X}
is never expanded.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-store " \fISTORE\fP"
The store to change (required).
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-file " \fIPATH\fP|\fB\-\fP"
Input file;
.B \-
or none reads stdin.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-type " \fBdotenv\fP|\fBjson\fP|\fBtoml\fP"
File format; inferred from the file name when omitted, dotenv for stdin.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-overwrite
Overwrite existing variables; otherwise the conflicts are listed and nothing
is written.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-key " \fIPATH\fP|\fB\-\fP"
Key or passphrase file of the store,
.B \-
for stdin.
.SS export
.B envs export
.RB [ \-f
.IR PATH | \- ]
.RB [ \-t
.BR dotenv | json | toml ]
.RB [ \-\-force ]
.RB [ \-\-nested ]
.RB [ \-\-first\-wins ]
.RB [ \-k
.IR STORE = PATH ]...
.RI [ STORE ...]
.PP
Export the merged stores as dotenv, JSON or TOML.
Writes to stdout unless
.B \-f
names a file (mode 0600).
Dotenv values are quoted so that python\-dotenv and Docker Compose read them
back unchanged; a value that cannot be written safely is refused with a hint
to use
.BR "\-t json" ,
.B "\-t toml"
or
.BR run .
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-file " \fIPATH\fP|\fB\-\fP"
Output file;
.B \-
or none writes to stdout.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-type " \fBdotenv\fP|\fBjson\fP|\fBtoml\fP"
File format; inferred from the file name
.RB ( .env* ,
.BR .json ,
.BR .toml )
when omitted, dotenv for stdout.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Replace an existing output file.
.TP
.B \-\-nested
Do not merge; keep stores apart: one table per store (JSON, TOML) or
.IB STORE __ NAME
(dotenv), with the store name in upper case and
.B \-
replaced by
.BR _ .
.TP
.B \-\-first\-wins
The first store wins instead of the last.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-key " \fISTORE\fP=\fIPATH\fP"
Key or passphrase file of a store; repeatable.
.SS export-shell
.B envs export\-shell
.RB [ \-o ]
.RB [ \-\-nested ]
.RB [ \-\-first\-wins ]
.RB [ \-k
.IR STORE = PATH ]...
.RI [ STORE ...]
.PP
Print
.B export
statements that load the merged stores into a POSIX shell (bash, zsh,
dash, ...).
A program cannot change its parent shell, so the output is evaluated:
.B eval \(dq$(envs export\-shell STORE...)\(dq.
Variables already set in the shell are kept unless
.B \-o
is given.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-overwrite
Overwrite variables that are already set in the shell.
.TP
.B \-\-nested
Prefix each variable with its store name:
.IR STORE __ NAME .
.TP
.B \-\-first\-wins
The first store wins instead of the last.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-key " \fISTORE\fP=\fIPATH\fP"
Key or passphrase file of a store; repeatable.
.SS run
.B envs run
.RB [ \-o ]
.RB [ \-\-nested ]
.RB [ \-\-first\-wins ]
.RB [ \-k
.IR STORE = PATH ]...
.RI [ STORE ...]
.B \-\-
.I CMD
.RI [ ARG ...]
.PP
Run
.I CMD
with the merged variables added to the current environment.
Everything after the first
.B \-\-
is the command, passed on untouched.
The exit status is the command's; on POSIX the process is replaced, so
signals reach the command directly.
Works in every shell, including fish and PowerShell.
Every
.B ENV_STORE_KEY_*
variable is removed from the command's environment, so it never inherits
a key.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-overwrite
Store values overwrite variables already set in the environment.
.TP
.B \-\-nested
Prefix each variable with its store name:
.IR STORE __ NAME .
.TP
.B \-\-first\-wins
The first store wins instead of the last.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-key " \fISTORE\fP=\fIPATH\fP"
Key or passphrase file of a store; repeatable.
.SS keygen
.B envs keygen
.IR PATH | \-
.PP
Generate a random 256\-bit key file at
.I PATH
(mode 0600, never overwritten).
.B envs keygen \-
prints the key instead, for example for a password manager.
Opens no store and needs no connection.
.SS rekey
.B envs rekey
.B \-s
.I STORE
.RB [ \-k
.IR PATH | \- ]
.RB ( \-\-new\-key
.IR PATH | \-
.RB | " \-\-generate\-key"
.I PATH
.RB | " \-\-passphrase" )
.PP
Re\-encrypt an encrypted store with a new secret; the new key file or
passphrase replaces the old one, and passphrases get a new salt.
Can switch between key file and passphrase.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-store " \fISTORE\fP"
The store to change (required).
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-key " \fIPATH\fP|\fB\-\fP"
The current key or passphrase file,
.B \-
for stdin.
Default:
.BI ENV_STORE_KEY_ STORE\fR,
then
.B [keys]
in the project file, else a prompt on a terminal.
The new secret never comes from the environment or
.BR [keys] .
.TP
.BR \-\-new\-key " \fIPATH\fP|\fB\-\fP"
Encrypt with this existing key file.
.TP
.BI \-\-generate\-key " PATH"
Encrypt with a new key file written to
.I PATH
(mode 0600, never overwritten).
.TP
.B \-\-passphrase
Encrypt with a passphrase: read from stdin when piped, else asked twice.
.SS encrypt
.B envs encrypt
.B \-s
.I STORE
.RB ( \-\-new\-key
.IR PATH | \-
.RB | " \-\-generate\-key"
.I PATH
.RB | " \-\-passphrase" )
.PP
Turn a plaintext store into an encrypted one.
There is no way back in place; a plaintext copy is made with
.BR "cp\-store \-\-plain" .
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-store " \fISTORE\fP"
The store to change (required).
.TP
.BR \-\-new\-key " \fIPATH\fP|\fB\-\fP"
Encrypt with this existing key file.
.TP
.BI \-\-generate\-key " PATH"
Encrypt with a new key file written to
.I PATH
(mode 0600, never overwritten).
.TP
.B \-\-passphrase
Encrypt with a passphrase: read from stdin when piped, else asked twice.
.SS tui
.B envs tui
.PP
Start the terminal user interface: browse, create, remove and export stores;
view, add, edit and delete variables.
Needs the
.B tui
extra.
Every screen's footer lists its keys; on the stores screen:
.TP
.B a
Create a store, with every encryption option of
.BR create\-store .
.TP
.B d
Remove the selected store, after its name is typed exactly; needs no key.
.TP
.B x
Export stores to a file, like
.BR export :
choose the stores in order, the format and options, then the path.
An existing file is only replaced after a confirmation; files get mode 0600.
.TP
.B Tab
In a path input (the export path and the key file fields), accept the
suggested path completion; otherwise move to the next field.
A leading
.I ~
is expanded and directories end in
.IR / .
.SS completion
.B envs completion
.BR bash | zsh | fish
.PP
Print the shell completion script; envs never edits shell start\-up files.
Add
.B eval \(dq$(envs completion zsh)\(dq
to
.I ~/.zshrc
(after
.BR compinit ),
.B eval \(dq$(envs completion bash)\(dq
to
.I ~/.bashrc
(bash 4.4 or newer; the bash 3.2 of macOS cannot run it, a Homebrew bash can), or write
.B envs completion fish
to
.IR ~/.config/fish/completions/envs.fish .
Run as
.BR environment\-store ,
the script completes that name.
.PP
It completes commands, options and fixed option values, existing store names
wherever an existing store is expected (never for a new name such as
.B create\-store
or the target of
.B cp\-store
and
.BR mv\-store ),
variable names of plaintext stores for
.BR get ,
.B rm
and
.BR set ,
and paths through the shell's own file completion.
The backend is the one the command would use:
.B \-c
on the line,
.B ENV_STORE_CONNECTION
or the project file.
Encrypted stores complete no variable names: completion never reads a key.
It never prompts, never prints to stderr, never creates or changes a file,
directory or schema, and completes nothing when the backend is missing,
unreachable or slow.
.SH STORE SELECTION AND KEYS
Reading commands
.RB ( ls ,
.BR get ,
.BR export ,
.BR export\-shell ,
.BR run )
take the stores as positional arguments; left to right is the load order.
Without stores they use
.B stores
from the project file.
Their keys are bound by store name:
.BI \-k " STORE" = PATH\fR,
repeatable;
.BI \-k " STORE" =\-
reads that store's key from stdin.
.B \-k
for a store that is not in the list, or that is not encrypted, is an error.
.PP
Writing commands
.RB ( set ,
.BR rm ,
.BR import ,
.BR rekey ,
.BR encrypt )
act on exactly one store, named with
.BR \-s ;
they never take it from the project file.
Their key is
.BI \-k " PATH"
or
.BR "\-k \-" .
Commands that act on a whole store
.RB ( create\-store ,
.BR rm\-store ,
.BR cp\-store ,
.BR mv\-store )
take its name as a positional argument.
.PP
A key file holds one line,
.BR envstore\-key\-v1: \fIbase64url\fP.
For a passphrase store the file given to
.B \-k
holds the passphrase; one trailing newline is stripped.
Without
.BR \-k ,
an encrypted store's key comes from
.BI ENV_STORE_KEY_ STORE\fR,
else from
.B [keys]
in the project file (see
.BR ENVIRONMENT ).
An encrypted store without a key is asked for with a hidden prompt on a
terminal, once per store; without a terminal, or with
.BR \-\-noinput ,
the command fails with exit code 4.
Only one
.B \-k \-
per command reads stdin.
.SH MERGE ORDER
By default the last store wins: a variable present in several stores takes
the value of the last store in the list that has it.
.B \-\-first\-wins
selects first wins instead, where a variable keeps the value of the first
store that has it.
It is the only command\-line override; there is no
.BR \-\-last\-wins .
When
.B ENV_STORE_ORDERED_OVERWRITE
or the project file selects first wins, last wins comes back by unsetting or
changing that setting.
Precedence:
.B \-\-first\-wins
>
.B ENV_STORE_ORDERED_OVERWRITE
>
.B ordered_overwrite
in the project file > default (last wins).
.PP
Merged output lists the variables in store load order.
Within one store they are sorted by name (code point order).
A variable that appears in several stores keeps the position of its first
appearance and takes the value chosen by the merge order.
This holds for
.BR ls ,
.BR export
(every format),
.B export\-shell
and
.BR run .
With store
.B a
holding
.B ENV=development
and
.BR DEBUG=True ,
and store
.B b
holding
.B ENV=staging
and
.BR DB_NAME=test ,
.B envs export a b
prints:
.PP
.RS
.nf
DEBUG=True
ENV=staging
DB_NAME=test
.fi
.RE
.PP
.B \-\-nested
does not merge at all; every store keeps its own namespace.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B ENV_STORE_CONNECTION
Connection string of the backend, as for
.BR \-c .
Precedence:
.B \-c
>
.B ENV_STORE_CONNECTION
>
.B connection
in the project file.
.TP
.B ENV_STORE_ORDERED_OVERWRITE
Merge order:
.B true
(default, last wins) or
.B false
(first wins).
Also accepts
.BR 1 ,
.BR 0 ,
.BR yes ,
.BR no ,
in any case.
.TP
.BI ENV_STORE_KEY_ STORE
Key line or passphrase of an existing encrypted store;
.I STORE
is the store name in upper case with
.B \-
turned into
.BR _ ,
e.g.
.B ENV_STORE_KEY_CUSTOMER_A
for
.BR customer\-a .
One trailing newline is stripped.
The value is the key file's content as it is, not base64\-encoded.
Precedence:
.B \-k
> this variable >
.B [keys]
in the project file > the prompt.
It is ignored for plain stores and for stores the command does not use,
and never used for new keys.
Two encrypted stores of one command that share the variable are an error
(exit code 1) unless
.B \-k
gives the key of both; an empty or invalid value is a key error (exit code 4)
that names the variable, never its value.
.B run
removes every
.B ENV_STORE_KEY_*
variable from the command's environment.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B .envstore.toml
Optional project file, searched in the current directory and then in its
parents; the first one found is used, unless
.B \-\-no\-project
is given.
Keys:
.B stores
(default store list for reading commands, in load order),
.B [keys]
(key file path per encrypted store, relative to the file's directory,
.B ~
expanded; paths only, never key material),
.B ordered_overwrite
(boolean) and
.B connection
(a connection string; a password in it causes a warning).
Stores given on the command line replace the file's list;
.B \-k
on the command line, and
.BI ENV_STORE_KEY_ STORE\fR,
override the file's key for that store.
.RS
.PP
.nf
stores = ["base_conf", "customer_a"]

[keys]
customer_a = "~/.keys/a.key"
.fi
.RE
.TP
.IB directory / store .json\fR,\fP " directory" / store .toml
One file per store in a
.B json://
or
.B toml://
backend.
Directories are created with mode 0700, store files with mode 0600.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Usage or validation error, including a refused schema setup and a missing
confirmation.
.TP
.B 2
Not found: a store or a variable.
.TP
.B 3
Already exists.
.TP
.B 4
Decryption or key error: wrong key, a missing key in non\-interactive use,
or an empty or invalid
.BI ENV_STORE_KEY_ STORE\fR.
.TP
.B 5
Concurrent modification: the store changed since it was read; nothing was
written, run the command again.
.PP
.B run
exits with the status of the command it runs.
.SH EXAMPLES
Use a directory of JSON files for the session:
.PP
.RS
.nf
export ENV_STORE_CONNECTION=json://~/.envstore
.fi
.RE
.PP
Create a plaintext store and an encrypted one with a new key file:
.PP
.RS
.nf
envs create\-store base_conf
envs create\-store \-e \-\-generate\-key a.key customer_a
.fi
.RE
.PP
Set plaintext values, and a secret from a file:
.PP
.RS
.nf
envs set \-s base_conf DB_HOST=localhost DB_PORT=5432
envs set \-s customer_a \-k a.key DB_PASSWORD < secret.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
List the merged variables with their values and sources:
.PP
.RS
.nf
envs ls \-v \-\-source base_conf customer_a \-k customer_a=a.key
.fi
.RE
.PP
Write a dotenv file, load the stores into the shell, or run a program:
.PP
.RS
.nf
envs export \-f .env base_conf customer_a
eval "$(envs export\-shell base_conf customer_a)"
envs run base_conf customer_a \-\- python app.py
.fi
.RE
.PP
Run once without installing, with uv
(until 2.0 is on PyPI, from the repository):
.PP
.RS
.nf
uvx \-\-from "environment\-store[cli] @ git+https://github.com/mkaufhol/environment\-store@feature/v2" envs ls\-store
.fi
.RE
Deploy with the key in the environment, for example from a Heroku config var or
a Kubernetes Secret;
.B run
removes it before starting the program:
.PP
.RS
.nf
export ENV_STORE_KEY_CUSTOMER_A="$(cat a.key)"
envs \-\-noinput run base_conf customer_a \-\- gunicorn app.wsgi
.fi
.RE
.SH SECURITY
Encryption covers the whole store: names and values.
Only the store name and metadata stay visible, so
.B ls
needs the key too.
A ciphertext is bound to its store name.
.PP
Keys are never accepted as literal arguments, and never stored or cached.
An existing store's key may come from
.BI ENV_STORE_KEY_ STORE\fR;
encryption then protects the backend and its backups, not against someone who
can read the deployment's configuration.
A key file mounted and passed with
.B \-k
or
.B [keys]
keeps the key out of the process environment.
Pass a key file with
.BR \-k ,
pipe it with
.BR "\-k \-" ,
or type it at the prompt.
.PP
.B set
refuses
.IR KEY = VALUE
for encrypted stores, because command lines end up in the shell history and
in
.BR ps (1);
pipe the value or use the prompt.
.PP
Key files and exported files are created with mode 0600; key files are never
overwritten.
Exported dotenv files and the output of
.B export\-shell
contain plaintext secrets.
Do not commit a project file whose
.B connection
contains a password; set
.B ENV_STORE_CONNECTION
instead.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR env (1),
.BR sh (1)
.PP
Documentation: https://github.com/mkaufhol/environment\-store
